course: everyday
lesson: 346
title: Teasing and laughing at others
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Teasing often starts as showing off. Maya in Denver called a girl «four-eyes» because her friends laughed. The girl cried in the toilets, the teacher found out, and Maya missed golden time (the free play time at the end of the week) and wrote a letter. Then she said sorry to the girl's face and told her friends it wasn't funny — that was the hard part, and the part that mattered.
explain: Some teasing is never punished. Maya's friend Josh copied the way a boy talks with his stammer, and everyone laughed. No teacher heard. But Josh saw the boy's face and felt horrible, and the boy didn't come to his party. Josh told it at confession, felt lighter afterwards, and then did what the priest asked: said sorry to the boy and stopped others doing it.
explain: Leaving someone out hurts as much as a push. The new boy stood by the fence all playtime because «the game is full». When the teacher made them include him, he turned out to be the best player they had.
explain: Laughing at a fall, a stammer, glasses, someone's food or a wrong answer makes people stop trying, stop bringing their lunch, stop putting their hand up. A good joke is one everyone laughs at, including the person it is about.
fact: Laughing at how someone looks, speaks or moves can hurt them for a long time.
fact: A stammer is a difference in speech that people cannot just switch off, and mocking it makes it harder.
fact: Leaving someone out on purpose hurts as much as a push.
fact: Food from another family or culture is not weird, only new to you.
fact: Inviting the whole class except one person is crueller than inviting just a few friends.
fact: Whispering unkind things behind someone's back is as bad as saying them out loud.
fact: A joke is only a joke if the person it is about laughs too.
fact: Saying sorry to the person's face and stopping others from teasing are both part of putting it right.
fact: Teasing is often done to show off or to fit in with a group.
fact: Confession can ease guilt about teasing, but the sorry to the person teased is still needed.
exercise: Why did Maya call the girl «four-eyes»? | To show off to her friends | Because she thought glasses were rude | The girl asked her to say it | She didn't know it was unkind at all
exercise: What happened after the girl cried in the toilets? | The teacher found out and Maya missed golden time | The girl stopped wearing her glasses to school after that | Nothing, as nobody saw | Maya got a star
exercise: Which part of Maya's putting right was hardest and mattered most? | Saying sorry to the girl's face and telling her friends it wasn't funny | Writing the girl a sorry letter and leaving it on her desk without saying anything | Missing golden time | Buying the girl a sweet
exercise: Why is copying a stammer especially unfair? | The boy can't switch his stammer off, and mocking makes it harder | He could talk normally if he really tried, so copying him helps him to try | Stammers are funny to everyone | It's only acting
exercise: Josh was never punished for copying the stammer. What happened anyway? | He felt horrible, and the boy didn't come to his party | He became more popular with the boys who laughed along | He forgot it at once | The boy thanked him
exercise: What did the priest ask Josh to do after confession? | Say sorry to the boy and stop others teasing him | Nothing more, it was all forgiven at confession | Pray for an hour | Stay away from that boy
exercise: The new boy stood alone by the fence because «the game is full». What is the kind fix? | Let him in and pass him the ball | Tell him to find his own game | Wait until he asks three times | Let him play only in goal
exercise: A girl's friend says her packed lunch «smells weird». She starts bringing only bread. What does this show? | Teasing changed what she ate and made her hungry and sad | She preferred plain bread anyway, so the friend was really helping her | Smells don't matter | The friend was helping her
exercise: Why is it crueller to invite the whole class except one boy? | He is the only one left out, so it's plainly about him | Big parties are always crueller, because more people know | It isn't crueller at all | He would not have come anyway
exercise: A boy laughs when another falls in PE and hurts his knee. What would be kind next time? | Help him up and ask if he's okay | Laugh more quietly so he can't hear | Pretend not to see | Copy the fall for a laugh
exercise: A girl whispers mean things about another girl, who hears. Is whispering better than saying it aloud? | No, it is just as bad | Yes, because it's quieter | Yes, if you're in a group | Yes, if she didn't hear every word
exercise: A boy calls his little sister stupid when she can't read a word. What happens? | She stops reading to him | She reads faster | She laughs | Nothing at all
exercise: A group says one girl can't join. What is the wise choice for one member? | Ask her to join, even if the others grumble | Stay quiet to keep the group happy and together | Leave her a note saying sorry | Start another group without her
exercise: How can you tell if a joke is a real joke? | The person it's about laughs too | Most people in the room laugh | You laugh the loudest | Nobody gets in trouble
exercise: Why do children often tease? | To show off or fit in with a group | Because the other child deserves it | Because teasing is helpful | To make the teacher happy
test: Mate: «it's only banter, she knows we're joking about her specs». She cried in the toilets. What's true? | It hurt her, so it wasn't a joke | It's banter so it's fine | She should toughen up | Specs jokes don't count
test: «everyone copied his stammer and nobody got told off so it's fine». What's right? | It still hurt him, and it's still wrong | It's fine if nobody's told off by a teacher | It's fine if he laughs a bit | It only matters in class
test: Josh at confession: «I made fun of a boy's stammer.» Father: «And what will you do about the boy?» What is the right answer? | Say sorry to him and stop the others | Nothing, I've told you now, that's enough | Avoid him | Copy him less often
test: Teacher: «He's been by the fence every playtime. Who said the game was full?» What should the boys do? | Own up and let him play | Say he didn't want to play | Say the game really was full | Say the teacher should play with him
test: Mum: «Why isn't Amir on the party list? You've asked everyone else». What fixes it best? | Say sorry to Amir and invite him | Say he annoys everyone at school | Cancel the party | Say he wouldn't fit
test: «look at her lunch, stinks lol» – said loudly at the table. What would be kind instead? | Ask what it is and maybe try some | Move to another table, away from the smell | Hold your nose | Laugh with the others
test: Sister, upset: «I'm not reading to you any more, you said I'm stupid». What helps most? | Say sorry and help her read slowly | Say she is a bit slow at reading | Read for her | Ignore her
test: A boy laughs when his classmate trips in PE. «Lighten up, it was funny». What is true? | Laughing at his fall hurts him, so it's no joke | It's always funny when people fall, everyone laughs | He should have laughed too | It's fine if he didn't cry
test: Group leader: «Lily can't join, we said so». What is the wise thing for a group member? | Say Lily can join, and ask her | Agree with the leader to keep the peace | Tell Lily it's the leader's fault | Leave the group in secret the next day
test: Which is TRUE about whispering mean things? | It's as hurtful as saying them aloud | It's harmless because it's very quiet | It's fine if she doesn't hear all of it | It's kinder than saying them out loud
test: Teacher's note: «Maya called a classmate names about her glasses». What did Maya do that mattered most? | Said sorry to the girl's face and stood up for her | Signed the sorry note and gave it to the teacher | Stayed in at golden time | Bought new glasses
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the mockery of a speech impediment. | It causes distress and may render the impediment more severe | It assists the person to speak more fluently, by drawing attention to the difficulty | It is harmless where intended humorously | It is of no consequence where unobserved by teachers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the exclusion of a single pupil from an invitation extended to an entire class is especially hurtful. | It singles out that pupil as the only person excluded | It reduces the number of guests unduly and spoils the occasion | It is contrary to the law | It is in no respect hurtful
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a remark constituting a genuine jest? | It is one at which the person concerned is also amused | It is any remark at which a majority laughs | It is any remark made without malicious intent | It is any remark not overheard by its subject
exam: Determine what is required of a pupil who has received absolution for the mockery of a classmate. | An apology to the classmate and the discouragement of further mockery by others | A written report to the head teacher only, the classmate being left without any apology | No further action of any description | The avoidance of the classmate
